## 🧩 Macro System (Component‑Level Abstraction)

HTML’s `<template>` + shadow DOM is verbose. MaxUp macros are **compile‑time expansions** that feel like writing normal code.

Syntax:
```
;;; macro card{title, image, link}
; .card
  %{src="{{ image }}" alt="{{ title }}"}
  ; .card-body
    #3 {{ title }}
    @{href="{{ link }}"} "Read more"
;;;

;;; macro button-group{buttons}
; .btn-group
  ;;; for b in buttons
  +{type="button"} {{ b }}
  ;;; end
;;;
```

Macro definition is a named block with parameters. Usage:
```
::: section#blog
{{ card{title="Sunset", image="sunset.jpg", link="/post1"} }}
{{ card{title="Forest", image="forest.jpg", link="/post2"} }}
:::
```

**Compilation**:  
The parser, when hitting a macro invocation, loads the macro’s AST, substitutes parameters, and flattens it into the current node. This works exactly like a preprocessor but with full AST awareness (so indentation nesting is correct).

Implementation plan:
- New token: `;;;` introduces macro definition or logic blocks.
- Parser already recognizes `;;;` as special; extend `Parser` to store a `HashMap<String, MacroDef>`.
- When parsing a `{{ macro{...} }}`, emit a `MacroExpansion` AST node. The emitter then resolves it by traversing the macro’s template AST with substituted values.

**Outpacing HTML**:  
No `<template>`, no `<slot>`, no imperative JS. Pure declarative composition — yet compiles to vanilla HTML. This is what the web platform should have been.

